# `:g/pat/normal …` reports once, for the whole `:g` — the per-line
# sub-command output is suppressed inside `:g`, as Vim does, so the
# summary is not buried under one `:norm — 1 line(s)` per line.
write a.txt "a1\na2\na3\na4\na5\na6\na7\na8\na9\n"
open a.txt
command editor.use_vim

type ":g/a/norm A;"
key enter
expect screen contains ":g — ran on 9 line(s)"
expect screen lacks ":norm — 1 line(s)"
expect screen lacks "more…"
